{"suite": "livecodebench", "question_id": "leetcode_maximum-binary-tree-ii", "prompt": "# Maximum Binary Tree II\n\nA **maximum tree** is a tree where every node has a value greater than any other value in its subtree. You are given the `root` of a maximum binary tree and an integer `val`.", "expected": "def ", "dataset_source": "hf_adapter", "prompt_hash": "d2ce4147d6d6", "timestamp": "2026-03-05T00:08:26.988531+00:00", "role_results": {"frontdoor:direct": {"role": "frontdoor", "mode": "direct", "answer": "The solution should be efficient in both time and space complexity.\n\n# Intuition\n\nThe key insight is that we're essentially inserting a new value into the maximum tree, which means we need to find where it fits according to the rules of constructing such trees.\n\n# Approach\n\n1. If `val` is greater than the root's value, then `val` becomes the new root.\n2. Otherwise, recursively insert `val` into the right subtree because:\n   - The new element will be appended at the end of array `a`.\n   - So it can only affect elements to its right in the tree structure.\n\n# Complexity\n\n- **Time complexity:** O(n) where n is the number of nodes.\n- **Space complexity:** O(h) where h is the height of the tree due to recursion stack.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Optional\n\nclass TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = left\n        self.right = right\n\ndef insertIntoMaxTree(root: Optional[TreeNode], val: int) -> Optional[TreeNode]:\n    if not root or val > root.val:\n        new_root = TreeNode(val)\n        new_root.left = root\n        return new_root\n    \n    root.right = insertIntoMaxTree(root.right, val)\n    return root\n```", "passed": true, "elapsed_seconds": 8.453952946001664, "error": null, "error_type": "none", "tokens_generated": 278, "tokens_generated_estimate": 0, "backend_task_id": 0, "slot_progress_source": "", "tools_used": 0, "tools_called": [], "delegation_events": [], "de